  <!-- REP-103 scan frame: X out the front of the scanner, Y left, Z up. Same problem
       and same fix as camera_link above — the exporter's lidar frames are CAD-style
       (Z forward, Y up), so pointing the driver at one would tip every range reading
       90 deg out of the floor plane. The driver's frame_id is set to this instead.
       Rotation only; the origin stays on lidar1_link, the rotating head, as the closest
       thing the CAD offers to the beam origin (7.3 cm ahead of base_link, 24 cm up, on
       centreline).
       The rpy carries TWO things. The first is the CAD-to-REP-103 correction, which on
       its own works out to exactly zero net rotation against base_link. The second is a
       180 deg yaw, because the scanner is physically MOUNTED BACKWARDS: measured
       2026-07-30 against a room with a known-asymmetric layout, a feature 8.5 m away in
       front of the robot was reported at 180 deg, and obstacles 0.35 m off the robot's
       left were reported on the right. Fitting bearing = s*reported + offset gives
       s = +1, offset = 180 deg, so the scan is NOT mirrored (driver stays
       inverted: false) and this is a pure yaw. Composing that yaw is what flips both
       signs relative to the CAD-only correction (0 -pi/2 -pi/2).
       Check it with `tf2_echo base_link laser`: it must read RPY 0 0 +/-pi with
       translation (0.073, 0, 0.241). Anything else means the chain is broken. -->
  <link
    name="laser" />
  <joint
    name="lidar1_to_laser"
    type="fixed">
    <origin
      xyz="0 0 0"
      rpy="0 1.5707963267949 1.5707963267949" />
    <parent
      link="lidar1_link" />
    <child
      link="laser" />
    <axis
      xyz="0 0 0" />
  </joint>
